WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:04.209
Before we start, let's go over some basic concepts.

2
00:00:05.309 --> 00:00:06.910
What are Blueprints?

3
00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:12.779
Well, Blueprints is Unreal Engine's visual scripting language.

4
00:00:12.779 --> 00:00:15.820
Now, it's important to know how this came about.

5
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.660
Unreal Engine runs off of a programming language called C++,

6
00:00:20.699 --> 00:00:26.699
and C++ is text-based and relatively complicated to learn.

7
00:00:27.339 --> 00:00:31.179
Now, when you're working on a large game or any other program,

8
00:00:31.739 --> 00:00:36.219
there is a likelihood that you're going to have a mix of people that are going to be working on it,

9
00:00:36.219 --> 00:00:38.539
and not all of them are going to be programmers.

10
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:42.219
So this is how Blueprints were born.

11
00:00:42.219 --> 00:00:45.420
They are exposed pieces of code,

12
00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:52.619
which can be placed within a flowchart-based graphical interface

13
00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:56.060
that allows you all of the power of C++,

14
00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:03.659
just in a much simpler, more graphical way of understanding.

15
00:01:03.659 --> 00:01:09.019
So when you've got level designers, or artists, or texture artists,

16
00:01:09.019 --> 00:01:11.019
they don't have to go away and learn C++.

17
00:01:12.379 --> 00:01:14.700
Originally, it was relatively limited,

18
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.420
and only a few nodes were actually exposed to the Blueprint system.

19
00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:23.420
But over the years, it's grown to be a lot more robust.

20
00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:29.900
At this point, there are some computer games that are almost entirely coded within Blueprints.

21
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:35.260
It runs, in some cases, slower than C++.

22
00:01:36.379 --> 00:01:42.299
However, for most people's purposes, it is sort of unnoticeable,

23
00:01:43.819 --> 00:01:45.019
unless you do something wrong.

24
00:01:46.659 --> 00:01:51.060
Now, C++ is an object-oriented programming language.

25
00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:55.299
So what does object-oriented programming language mean?

26
00:01:55.859 --> 00:02:03.219
Well, in simple terms, it means that all of the code is split out into objects,

27
00:02:03.219 --> 00:02:06.420
and these can contain both data and functionality,

28
00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:12.979
but it's designed to make everything easier to manage and reuse.

29
00:02:12.979 --> 00:02:17.060
So you don't have to create hundreds and hundreds of different pieces of code.

30
00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:21.300
You can just reuse the same objects, but in slightly different ways.

31
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:27.539
Within the engine, we will talk about Blueprints,

32
00:02:27.539 --> 00:02:30.180
and we'll talk about classes, and we'll talk about instances.

33
00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:37.060
Now, a class serves as a template for something that you want to create,

34
00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:39.379
and can be seen in the Content Browser.

35
00:02:39.379 --> 00:02:40.900
So you can see it here in pink.

36
00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:43.139
We have the third person character.

37
00:02:43.139 --> 00:02:46.740
This is the class, the Blueprint.

38
00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:51.139
But if you were to drag this into the world, it would create an instance.

39
00:02:51.139 --> 00:02:54.979
You can see three of them here, depicted by the blue arrows.

40
00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:59.939
An instance is what you get when you drag a class into the world.

41
00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:04.979
Now, each of these instances can have its own settings and variables,

42
00:03:04.979 --> 00:03:07.460
even though it originates from the same class.

43
00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:13.379
And we're going to work on this here in the exercise a bit more.

44
00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:17.939
But if you imagine an NPC class or a enemy class,

45
00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:19.539
that might have...

46
00:03:20.259 --> 00:03:25.460
You could have hundreds of instances of the NPC within the world,

47
00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:27.780
but they might all have their own health,

48
00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:31.139
or they may all have their own strength, or some other different variable.

49
00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:35.780
But that needs to be tracked by instance,

50
00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:38.419
and not something that's going to be held within the class.

51
00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:42.879
The other concept that's important to understand is inheritance.

52
00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:46.800
When we're working with an object-orientated language,

53
00:03:47.439 --> 00:03:56.080
everything inherits functionality and sort of values from its parent class.

54
00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:02.240
So within Unreal Engine, we have, and especially within the editor,

55
00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.360
we have a class called Object, or within C++, it would be UObject.

56
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:13.360
Now, this is the highest class that you're going to work with.

57
00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:16.720
Within the editor, pretty much everything you'll ever work with

58
00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:19.279
will be a child of this object class.

59
00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:23.360
Now, this object class has some definite benefits to it.

60
00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:24.639
We'll go over them in a second.

61
00:04:25.439 --> 00:04:28.800
Any of the functionality and data of the object

62
00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.240
is then going to be given to a child class, in this case, such as an actor.

63
00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.759
And that can have extra functionality,

64
00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.600
and that will be provided to every child below it.

65
00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.720
But there's this concept of inheritance.

66
00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:46.480
You will be able to reuse the code from the parent class,

67
00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:50.000
and this makes it easier to manage and extend functionality,

68
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.639
as all the child classes, they can have additional features or code,

69
00:04:54.639 --> 00:04:59.040
or they can override the functionality that they had.

70
00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.639
Now, here's a good example case.

71
00:05:01.199 --> 00:05:04.160
If you jump into the engine and you're asked to pick a class,

72
00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:10.800
some of the most common classes are going to be listed as actor, pawn, and character.

73
00:05:11.439 --> 00:05:14.480
So first of all, we have the highest up class that we can have,

74
00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.079
and that's the object class.

75
00:05:16.639 --> 00:05:21.199
And the object base class in Unreal Engine is the highest up one,

76
00:05:21.199 --> 00:05:25.120
and it has some functionality that we're not going to worry about much here,

77
00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:29.199
but there are things like garbage collection and low-level coding,

78
00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:33.120
things to make life just simpler for you that you don't need to worry about.

79
00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:39.600
Now, a common class which is a child of the object class is the actor class.

80
00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:44.240
Now, the actor has the ability to be spawned into the world,

81
00:05:44.879 --> 00:05:47.680
complete with a transform for positioning.

82
00:05:48.579 --> 00:05:53.939
So an actor has the ability to exist within the viewport.

83
00:05:54.500 --> 00:06:00.339
Anything that is in the viewport will be a child of the actor class,

84
00:06:01.259 --> 00:06:05.259
and this has the transform attached to it.

85
00:06:05.259 --> 00:06:07.420
So you would see that in the details panel,

86
00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:11.740
but the transform just has a location, rotation, and scale

87
00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:15.259
that will allow placement in the world

88
00:06:15.819 --> 00:06:20.300
and will help us to be able to do things like render it out

89
00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:22.699
and figure out collisions and so on.

90
00:06:22.699 --> 00:06:27.100
The actor has the functionality of being able to exist in the world

91
00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:30.220
in a specific location in the level.

92
00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:33.089
Now we have the pawn class.

93
00:06:33.089 --> 00:06:35.569
The pawn class is a child of the actor class,

94
00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:41.490
and this has the power to be possessed and receive input from a controller.

95
00:06:42.959 --> 00:06:47.120
Now, this doesn't necessarily have to be a player.

96
00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:49.360
Usually it would often be a player controller,

97
00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:52.160
but it could also be an AI-controlled character.

98
00:06:52.959 --> 00:06:56.480
So it has the functionality to be possessed.

99
00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.560
But as a pawn, it is also inheriting all of the functionality of an actor.

100
00:07:02.639 --> 00:07:06.079
So it's getting the ability to exist somewhere in the world,

101
00:07:06.079 --> 00:07:09.439
and the actor is getting the functionality from the object.

102
00:07:09.439 --> 00:07:11.439
So things like garbage collection.

103
00:07:12.689 --> 00:07:15.329
Garbage collection is just cleaning up the memory

104
00:07:15.329 --> 00:07:18.129
as you go along so that the game runs smoothly.

105
00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:22.990
And the final class we're going to look at here is the character class.

106
00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:28.589
Now the character class includes a skeletal mesh and a movement component,

107
00:07:28.589 --> 00:07:32.350
allowing for an easy setup and animating a controllable character.

108
00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:37.329
So this has extra functionality, and they're actually components.

109
00:07:37.329 --> 00:07:41.170
So the character class has inherited from the pawn class.

110
00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:44.050
So the character class has got movement and skeletal mesh.

111
00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:47.490
But from the pawn class, it's got the ability to be possessed.

112
00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:51.730
And from the actor class, it has the ability to be placed within the world.

113
00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:55.889
And from the object class, it has got garbage collection.

114
00:07:55.889 --> 00:07:58.209
It's another low-level coding we need.

115
00:07:58.769 --> 00:08:00.449
This is how inheritance works.

116
00:08:00.449 --> 00:08:05.250
Whatever class you have can inherit functionality from its parents.

117
00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:06.370
It will by default.

118
00:08:07.009 --> 00:08:11.329
We are not going to go over in here, but you can also override functionality.

119
00:08:11.329 --> 00:08:13.170
So you can create a child class,

120
00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:17.569
which has actually got different coding from its parents.

121
00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:22.639
You can have multiple children of a singular class.

122
00:08:22.639 --> 00:08:28.160
So for example, in this case, the object class has an actor component and the actor class.

123
00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:30.000
But it could have lots and lots of different children,

124
00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.159
which all have different functionality.

125
00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:35.759
And then those below them can have more children of lots of different classes.

126
00:08:35.759 --> 00:08:39.600
So you can see how her system can get a lot more complicated.

127
00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:43.519
But because this is all object-based and class-based,

128
00:08:43.519 --> 00:08:51.200
it means if you write the code in the correct place and create children accordingly,

129
00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:53.759
then you won't have to do a lot of rewriting.

130
00:08:53.759 --> 00:08:57.440
You won't have to manage code in lots and lots of different places.

131
00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:02.240
You can just have the functionality you will call written in singular places.
